  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDeepening area of low pressure off the coast will combine with \na surface high pressure over the Great Lakes to produce \nincreasing northeast winds over central NY and northeast PA \nbeginning later tonight and continuing through the weekend. This\nNor'Easter, currently 300 miles east of Norfolk, VA, is \nexpected to take a meandering track to the north on Saturday and\nhug the NJ coast Sunday before moving northeastward across New \nEngland on Monday. \n\nThe east/west position of the track of the low has been a \nchallenge for the model guidance the past several days, but \nlooking at the trends of the ensemble members using a Cluster \nAnalysis indicates that a track closer to the coast is expected \nwith high certainty. The majority of the 12Z model guidance \narriving this morning is supporting this and the result is an \narea of widespread rain even farther west and inland than \npreviously anticipated. \n\nThe 13Z NBM probabilistic/long-term guidance, which includes \nthe 00Z ensemble suite, produces a realistic solution with a \nband of light rain lifting in from the southeast on Saturday \nafternoon and then dissipating in the evening and early \novernight hours as a fairly deep layer of dry air gets entrained\ninto a portion of the Nor'Easter over the interior Northeast. \n\nThe bulk of the rain for central NY and ne PA is expected to \nfall on Sunday as the system remains baroclinic and an embedded \nshort wave rotates around the northern edge of the low through \nNew England into NY and PA, bringing with it a solid batch of \nrain and winds. Probabilities for a quarter of an inch of rain \non Sunday support this amount over almost the entire forecast \narea, while prob of greater than a half inch of rain is \ngenerally focused in the Catskills and much of northeast PA. The\nheaviest amounts will likely be in the Poconos and higher \nelevations of the Catskills, where up to an inch is possible. \nThere continues to be hints of some decent shadowing of rain on \nthe western side of the Catskills. \n\nMeanwhile, wind gusts should top out around 20 to 30 mph for \nmost of the populated areas of central NY and ne PA, but some of\nthe higher elevations could see occasional 30 to 40 mph wind \ngusts through the weekend. \n\nThe event winds down later on Monday/Mon night as the low picks\nup speed and lifts to the northeast. Rain will come to an end \nMonday night and winds will subside. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nA weak ridge of high pressure builds in across the Northeast US\nTuesday and Wednesday, which will cause large scale suppression\nand usher in a gradually drying air mass. Skies should clear \nout Tuesday night and continue into Wednesday. A warmer air mass\nmoving in combined with plenty of sun on Wed will allow \ntemperatures to climb into the lower to mid 70s. \n\nThere does appear to be another open short wave that attempts \nto move in from the west Thu or Friday, but the timing and \nplacement of the boundary is still in question. If the wave \nholds back to the west, temperatures could climb again into the \n70s. However if more clouds and rain move in quicker, \ntemperatures could be substantially cooler.",
